# Coach speech (Slovenian): the building blocks every line uses (src/coach/catalog.cpp renders them).
# Same keys as assets/coach/speech/en/common.lang, plus the grammatical forms Slovenian needs.
# Standard Slovenian and the words of Slovenian chess books: kralj, dama, trdnjava, lovec, skakač,
# kmet; šah, mat, pat, remi, rokada; poteza (feminine!), polje, linija (file), vrsta (rank).
# Register: the coach says "ti" to the player (the game menus say "vi"). The coach is male ("pokazal
# sem", "vesel sem"). The player's gender is unknown, and every Slovenian past tense ("si vzel"),
# future ("boš vzel") and conditional ("bi vzel") shows it, as do adjectives ("pripravljen"): for the
# player use only the present (the perfective present for what comes next: "Če vzameš kmeta, ..."),
# the imperative, infinitives, impersonal turns ("uspelo ti je najti", "bilo je mogoče vzeti", "treba
# je") and noun subjects ("tvoja poteza je bila odlična"). Coach and player together are the dual
# "midva": "Poglejva", "Ponoviva"; "sva igrala" is always right (a pair with a man in it is masculine).

# ---- Pieces --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Nouns: kmet, skakač, lovec, kralj (m, animate: accusative = genitive, "vzemi mojega lovca"),
# trdnjava, dama (f). piece.<type>.gender is m or f; an owner pattern without a gender is the
# masculine one, ".f" the feminine one. Owners: your = tvoj, my = moj, and in the Analysis mode (nobody
# being "ti" or "jaz" there) white = beli, black = črni ("beli skakač", "črno damo").
# EVERY form below is defined for all six pieces and all four owners, so it works the same for
# {your} {your2} {my} {my2} {t1} {t2} and the Analysis {piece}. Never write tvoj, moj, svoj, beli, črni
# or ta before a piece placeholder: the form says it. The coach points at the piece on the FIRST
# placeholder of that piece in the line: name the piece (a case form) before its pronouns and endings.
#
# With the possessive, the six cases, and "with" (s / z + instrumental):
#   nom        tvoj skakač / tvoja dama            "{your} je pod napadom."  (the default, {your})
#   gen        tvojega skakača / tvoje dame        "Do {my:gen} imaš prosto pot."
#   dat        tvojemu skakaču / tvoji dami        "{my} grozi {your:dat}."
#   acc        tvojega skakača / tvojo damo        "Vzamem {your:acc}."  (animate: = genitive)
#   loc        tvojem skakaču / tvoji dami         "Razmisli o {my:loc}."
#   ins        tvojim skakačem / tvojo damo        "Za {your:ins} stoji {t1}."  (za + ins: behind)
#   with       s tvojim skakačem / z mojo damo     "Menjava {your:gen} {my:with} je zate slaba."
#              (the preposition is in the form: s tvojim, z mojim, z belim, s črnim; never write s/z)
# "svoj" when the owner of the piece is the subject of the clause (the player of an imperative or of
# a "ti" verb, the coach saying "jaz", a side, or the piece itself): Slovenian then says "svoj", never
# "tvoj" / "moj" ("Razvij svojega lovca", not "tvojega"). There is no nominative.
#   refl_gen   svojega skakača / svoje dame        "Pazi na varnost {your:refl_gen}."
#   refl_dat   svojemu skakaču / svoji dami        "Pomagaj {your:refl_dat}."
#   refl_acc   svojega skakača / svojo damo        "Razvij {your:refl_acc}.", "Izgubim {my:refl_acc}."
#   refl_loc   svojem skakaču / svoji dami         "Razmisli o {your:refl_loc}."
#   refl_ins   svojim skakačem / svojo damo        "Pred {your:refl_ins} imaš prosto pot."
#   refl_with  s svojim skakačem / s svojo damo    "Vzemi {my:acc} {your:refl_with}."
# Without a possessive (the kind of piece; "Vzemi ga s skakačem!" is often more natural):
#   bare       skakač / dama                       "{your:bare} se premika v obliki črke L."
#   bare_gen   skakača / dame                      "Pazi se {my:bare_gen}!"
#   bare_dat   skakaču / dami                      "Približaj se {my:bare_dat}."
#   bare_acc   skakača / damo                      "Vzemi {my:bare_acc}!"
#   bare_loc   skakaču / dami                      "Pri {your:bare_loc} pazi na vezavo."
#   bare_ins   skakačem / damo                     "Za {my:bare_ins} stoji {t1}."
#   bare_with  s skakačem / z damo (s / z included) "Vzemi {my:acc} {your:bare_with}!"
# Pronouns for the piece, the same words for every owner. Subject pronouns only for stress; the
# clitics (ga, je, jo, mu, ji) stand second in their clause; after a preposition the full forms:
#   pron       on / ona                            "Samo {your:pron} brani to polje."
#   pron_gen   njega / nje                         "Do {my:pron_gen} imaš prosto pot."
#   pron_dat   njemu / njej                        "Proti {my:pron_dat} nimaš obrambe."
#   pron_acc   njega / njo (stressed)              "Pazi na {your:pron_acc}, ne na {t1:acc}."
#   pron_loc   njem / njej                         "Na {my:pron_loc} sloni vsa obramba."
#   pron_ins   njim / njo                          "{your} je vezan{your:ag}: za {your:pron_ins} je {t1}."
#   cl_gen     ga / je (clitic, negation)          "{your} visi in ne moreš {your:cl_gen} rešiti."
#   cl_dat     mu / ji (clitic)                    "{your} je v nevarnosti: {my} {your:cl_dat} grozi."
#   cl_acc     ga / jo (clitic)                    "{my} visi. Vzemi {my:cl_acc}!"
#   pron_na    nanj / nanjo (= na + acc)           "{your} visi in {my} meri {your:pron_na}."
#   pron_za    zanj / zanjo (= za + acc)           "{my} je zastonj: nič ne daš {my:pron_za}."
#   pron_v     vanj / vanjo (= v + acc)            "{t1} je tarča: {my} udari {t1:pron_v}."
#   poss       njegov / njen: the stem of "its"; glue the ending of the possessed noun ("{your:poss}o
#              polje" = njegovo / njeno polje, "{your:poss}i kmeti", "{your:poss}ih kmetov"):
#              "{your} je zaprt{your:ag}: {your:poss}a polja so vsa napadena." When the piece is the
#              subject, "svoj" instead: "{your} je zaprt{your:ag} za svojimi kmeti."
# The relative "which" agreeing with the piece, after a preposition (plain "ki" + a clitic
# otherwise: "{my}, ki ga napadaš, ..."; "rel" itself is rare):
#   rel        kateri / katera                     (rare: the nominative is "ki": "{my}, ki brani {sq}")
#   rel_gen    katerega / katere                   "{my}, brez {my:rel_gen} se obramba sesuje, ..."
#   rel_dat    kateremu / kateri                   "{my}, proti {my:rel_dat} nimaš obrambe, ..."
#   rel_acc    katerega / katero                   "{your}, na {your:rel_acc} meri {my}, ..."
#   rel_loc    katerem / kateri                    "{my}, na {my:rel_loc} sloni obramba, ..."
#   rel_ins    katerim / katero                    "{t1}, za {t1:rel_ins} stoji {t2}, ..."
# Agreement endings (masculine / feminine piece): glue one to a word that agrees with the piece (a past
# tense, a predicate adjective or participle), after the piece itself has been named:
#   ag         "" / "a"   words that keep their stem: bil, imel, stal, ostal, visel, videl, branil,
#                         napadal, vzel; vezan, napaden, zaščiten, nezaščiten, izgubljen, ogrožen,
#                         ujet, zaprt, odprt, izpostavljen, prost, sam:
#                         "{my} je bil{my:ag} nezaščiten{my:ag}." = "Moj lovec je bil nezaščiten." /
#                         "Moja dama je bila nezaščitena."
#   ag_el      "el" / "la"  past tenses with a fleeting e: pad-, š-, priš-, uš-, mog-, napad-, rek-:
#                         "{t1} je pad{t1:ag_el}." = "padel" / "padla"
#   ag_en      "en" / "na"  adjectives in -en with a fleeting e: var-, vred-, nevar-, moč-, nemoč-,
#                         aktiv-, pasiv-, pomemb-: "{your} je vred{your:ag_en} {n:point_gen}."
#   ag_ek      "ek" / "ka"  adjectives in -ek: šib-, krat-, niz-: "{your} je šib{your:ag_ek}."
#   agi        "i" / "a"  definite adjectives and comparatives (cenejši, močnejši, vrednejši):
#                         "{my} je cenejš{my:agi} od {your:gen}." = "cenejši" / "cenejša"
#   Anything else (dober / dobra ...): rephrase, use the present ("{t1} pade", "{my} visi") or a
#   noun phrase ("{your} je brez zaščite", "je v vezavi", "je v pasti", "je na varnem").
# Two pieces at once ({t1} and {t2}, {your} and {your2}) take the dual. Present-tense dual verbs show
# no gender ("{t1} in {t2} sta pod napadom", "padeta"), but dual participles and adjectives do
# ("napadena" / "napadeni"), and so do "oba / obe" and "eden / ena": no form can agree with two
# different pieces. Say "sta pod napadom", "napadam {t1:acc} in {t2:acc} hkrati", or let a noun carry
# the agreement ("obe figuri sta napadeni: {t1} in {t2}", "ena od obeh figur pade"). Referring back to
# the pair needs no form: ju / njiju (acc), njiju / obeh (gen), jima / njima / obema (dat, ins), the
# same for every gender: "Ne moreš rešiti obeh."
